    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d1066">

                <pb facs="1323" n="1311" sameAs="frus:frus1945v05/pg1311"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">760h.75/7–545: Telegram</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Chargé in Yugoslavia</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic"
                        >Shantz</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of State </hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Belgrade</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1945-07-05T16:00:00+02:00">July 5, 1945—4
                            p.m.</date>
                        <lb/> [Received 7:30 p.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>233. Embtel No. 230 July 3.<anchor n="36" xml:id="d1066-f1"/> Conversations with
                    Albanian[s?] here fail to substantiate rumors re Yugo-Albanian federation.</p>
                <p>Acting Chief of the small Albanian Military Mission told Fraleigh<anchor n="37"
                        xml:id="d1066-f2"/> “Albania isn’t interested in federation but wants only
                    freedom and friendship with her neighbors and great allies”. He said trouble on
                    Greek border is largely a “newspaper war”; that Albania is not <pb facs="1324"
                        n="1312" xml:id="pg1312"/> concerned greatly about border problems, relying
                    on peace conference for fair settlement.</p>
                <p>Albanian Diplomatic Mission expected in Belgrade soon.</p>
                <p>Sent Dept, rptd Tirana and Caserta.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Shantz</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d1066-f1" n="36" xml:id="d1066-f1-content">
                <p>Not printed; this telegram informed the Department that Foreign Minister Subasich
                    denied the truth of rumors concerning an impending Albanian-Yugoslav federation
                    (760h.75/7–345).</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d1066-f2" n="37" xml:id="d1066-f2-content">
                <p>William N. Fraleigh, Second Secretary of Embassy and Vice Consul in
                    Yugoslavia.</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
    </standOff>
